Below is a list of the most important issues in your snippet, followed by a revised version and commentary.

1) You never validate your inputs or the server response  
   • If `productId` contains characters like “../” or user-supplied control characters, you can open yourself to unexpected routing or injection.  
   • You assume that `res.json()` returns a raw number. Most APIs actually return an object (e.g. `{ price: 12.95 }`), so you will end up storing `"[object Object]"` in localStorage and parsing it to `NaN`.  
   • You never check `res.ok`. A 404 or 500 will happily call `res.json()` (or throw an unrelated parsing error).

2) You have no error handling around fetch or localStorage  
   • If the network is down or the server is unreachable, your function will throw an un­caught exception.  
   • If `localStorage` is full, in private mode, or disabled by the user, `getItem`/`setItem` can throw.  

3) You cache forever with no expiration or invalidation  
   • If your price changes in the backend, you will never re-fetch it once it’s in localStorage.  

4) Storing in localStorage opens you up to XSS tampering  
   • Anything you store in localStorage can be overwritten by malicious scripts on the page.  

5) You do no deduplication for concurrent calls  
   • If two components both call `getPrice("123")` at the same time you will issue two network requests.

— ­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­  
Revised version with fixes, comments inline:

```js
// constants
const PRICE_CACHE_TTL = 1000 * 60 * 5;   // 5 minutes

// In-flight promise map to dedupe concurrent calls
const inFlight = new Map();

async function getPrice(productId) {
  // 1) Validate + normalize productId
  if (productId == null) {
    throw new TypeError("productId is required");
  }
  // coerce to string and encode
  const id = encodeURIComponent(String(productId).trim());
  if (!id) {
    throw new TypeError("productId must be a non-empty string or number");
  }

  const cacheKey = `price_${id}`;

  // 2) Deduplicate concurrent calls
  if (inFlight.has(id)) {
    return inFlight.get(id);
  }

  const promise = (async () => {
    let cached;
    // 3) Safe localStorage read
    try {
      const raw = localStorage.getItem(cacheKey);
      if (raw) {
        cached = JSON.parse(raw);
      }
    } catch (err) {
      console.warn("Could not read from localStorage", err);
    }

    // 4) Check TTL and type
    if (
      cached &&
      typeof cached.price === "number" &&
      Date.now() - cached.timestamp < PRICE_CACHE_TTL
    ) {
      return cached.price;
    }

    // 5) Fetch fresh from server
    let res;
    try {
      res = await fetch(`/api/price/${id}`, {
        method: "GET",
        credentials: "same-origin",
        headers: {
          "Accept": "application/json"
        }
      });
    } catch (networkErr) {
      throw new Error("Network error while fetching price: " + networkErr.message);
    }

    if (!res.ok) {
      // handle 4xx/5xx
      const text = await res.text().catch(() => "");
      throw new Error(`Failed to fetch price (${res.status}): ${text}`);
    }

    let json;
    try {
      json = await res.json();
    } catch (parseErr) {
      throw new Error("Invalid JSON from price endpoint: " + parseErr.message);
    }

    // 6) Validate server response shape
    if (typeof json.price !== "number") {
      throw new TypeError("API returned invalid price data");
    }

    // 7) Store in localStorage with timestamp
    try {
      localStorage.setItem(
        cacheKey,
        JSON.stringify({ price: json.price, timestamp: Date.now() })
      );
    } catch (storageErr) {
      // storage might be full or disabled
      console.warn("Could not write price to localStorage", storageErr);
    }

    return json.price;
  })();

  inFlight.set(id, promise);

  // ensure cleanup when done
  promise
    .catch(() => {})
    .then(() => inFlight.delete(id));

  return promise;
}
```

Key improvements:

• Input validation & `encodeURIComponent` to avoid path injection.  
• Check `res.ok` and throw helpful errors on network/server failures.  
• Validate the shape of the JSON you get back (`json.price`).  
• Wrap all `localStorage` calls in `try/catch` to handle quota or disabled storage.  
• Store a `{ price, timestamp }` object under the key and implement a TTL so your app can re-fetch stale data.  
• Use an in-flight map to dedupe concurrent requests for the same product.  
• Log (but don’t crash) on non-critical failures (e.g. cache misses or storage errors).  

Further considerations:  
• If prices are sensitive, localStorage can be tampered with by XSS—consider storing server-signed payloads or moving the cache to an HttpOnly cookie or secure IndexedDB layer.  
• If your app is large and you need a more robust cache, look into a small in-memory LRU cache or service-worker based cache.
